1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:16,160
Hey everyone!Recall how strategic bargaining plays out: we use normal and sequential games to see the strategies players choose. We also considered first-mover advantage and credible threats. Let's now turn our attention to non-strategic bargaining.

2
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:18,840
Let's start with a definition:

3
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:24,760
non-strategic bargaining focuses on outside options or disagreement values.

4
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,480
Well that doesn't help us too much.

5
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,200
What are outside options or disagreement value?

6
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:36,440
Imagine you're sitting in your house. What alternatives do you have?

7
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,760
You could be outside side where the grass in greener.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,760
In essence this is what we mean by outside options.

9
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,680
Do we have an opportunity cost with our current bargain?

10
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:56,320
If so, then we can use that as leverage. Remember the grass is always greener on the other side.

11
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,480
Here's Deb. Deb is a really good journalist.

12
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:07,680
But Deb wonders is there more out there for her. Can she find a better job than what she has.

13
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:18,120
Here's Steve. Steve is the current editor at Deb's news company. Steve wants to give Deb a raise. After all, she is a Pulitzer prize winner.

14
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:22,200
Steve plans to offer Deb $100,000

15
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:27,720
Under the strategic view of bargaining Deb can accept or reject the offer.

16
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:40,240
We could set up a game and solve for it. Or we could look at it another way. Suppose Deb isn't satisfied with the $100,000 offer and instead decides to negotiate.

17
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,000
This could turn into a repeated game.

18
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,720
But what if we wanted to end it without having to play multiple rounds?

19
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,160
This is where outside options come in.

20
00:01:52,720 --> 00:02:00,640
Let's say Deb has been looking for a new job for quite some time and has a very attractive offer from a competing newspaper.

21
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:06,640
Deb can use this outside option as a means to drive negotiations in her favor.

22
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:15,560
But what if Steve has several candidates lined up to replace Deb or to add to the existing cast of journalists.

23
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:19,040
Now Steve has the larger outside offer.

24
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:28,680
What do we see? Quite simply, you can improve your bargaining position by having an outside option or larger outside option.

25
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:35,400
We don't have to consider just changing jobs. We can bargain a lot in everyday life.

26
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:52,840
One area we may choose to bargain is insurance. We simply shop around for multiple quotes to build our inventory of outside options. If you have ever purchased a home, you likely did this when selecting your home insurer.

27
00:02:53,680 --> 00:03:00,720
We can also do this with our utilities at least the ones were there multiple companies such as cable and phones.

28
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:05,960
Let's conclude with a few other ways to improve your bargaining position.

29
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:19,040
First, is patience. If you are in a negotiation and the other party has a strict deadline, you might find yourself in a better bargaining position since they need to deal to close.

30
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:27,240
Do you own something that no one else has or are in a position that elevates your skill level above others?

31
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:33,160
If so you can improve your bargaining position think about your skills relative to your. Think about your skills relative to your coworkers.

32
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:36,840
Do you find yourself with a higher level of skills than them?

33
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:43,240
If so, then you may be more valuable to your company and can command a higher salary.

34
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,800
How reputable is a particular firm or person?

35
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:52,760
Do you know someone, or are that someone, that can close a deal?

36
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:59,000
If so, you/they probably have a reputation and this gives you bargaining power.

37
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:05,800
While not easy to obtain, if you can find out the other party's bottom line price

38
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,600
then you know the lower bound you can offer.

39
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:19,400
But be careful here. If you're too aggressive, the other party may simply walk away.

40
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:22,760
Lastly, let's consider signaling.

41
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:28,240
What can you tell other about yourself without explicitly stating it?

42
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:33,640
Think about getting an MBA. What exactly are you letting an employer know?

43
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:39,800
Quite simply that you are someone with an advanced degree and a higher quality applicant.

44
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,520
This signal can give you an edge in the job market.

45
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:46,200
Alright, that's it. Hope you've enjoyed it and we'll catch you in the next one.